      "entryAnswer": {
        "op": "submit",
        "snippet": "/**\n * B121 entry validation: hand a validated FullCausalCapability to a recipient.\n *\n * The recipient is invoked only when the supplied capability is the exact issued\n * object for this Graph and this expected CausalBinding. A structural copy, a\n * capability issued for another Graph, or one issued under a different binding\n * (contract / implementationRevision / scope / epoch) is refused before any\n * delivery, so no level is ever handed out from an unverified handle.\n *\n * The three levels are delivered as the original frozen handles, preserving the\n * identity relationships established at issuance:\n *   full.execution.identity === full.identity\n *   full.retained.execution === full.execution\n * (see packages/ts/src/solutions/causal-occurrence/capabilities.ts:88-89 and\n *  createCausalCapabilities at :122-146).\n */\nimport type { Graph } from \"../../packages/ts/src/graph/graph.js\";\nimport {\n\tassertCausalCapabilities,\n\ttype CausalBinding,\n\ttype ExecutionCapability,\n\ttype FullCausalCapability,\n\ttype IdentityCapability,\n\ttype RetainedEvidenceCapability,\n} from \"../../packages/ts/src/solutions/causal-occurrence/capabilities.js\";\n\n/** The three graded levels, delivered together and never re-wrapped. */\nexport interface CausalCapabilityRecipient<T> {\n\treadonly identity: IdentityCapability<T>;\n\treadonly execution: ExecutionCapability<T>;\n\treadonly retained: RetainedEvidenceCapability<T>;\n}\n\nexport type CausalCapabilityRecipientCallback<T> = (\n\tlevels: CausalCapabilityRecipient<T>,\n) => void;\n\n/**\n * Validate the supplied capability against the owning Graph and the expected\n * binding, then deliver its three levels to the recipient.\n *\n * Throws (and never calls the recipient) when the capability is not an issued\n * object, belongs to a different Graph, or was issued under a different binding.\n */\nexport function handoffCausalCapabilities<T>(\n\tgraph: Graph,\n\tcapabilities: FullCausalCapability<T>,\n\texpected: CausalBinding,\n\trecipient: CausalCapabilityRecipientCallback<T>,\n): void {\n\t// Exact issued-object + Graph + binding + lineage validation. A structural\n\t// copy or a copied lower handle fails here (capabilities.ts:66-90).\n\tassertCausalCapabilities(graph, capabilities, expected);\n\n\t// Deliver the original handles; do not rebuild or re-freeze the levels.\n\tconst levels: CausalCapabilityRecipient<T> = Object.freeze({\n\t\tidentity: capabilities.identity,\n\t\texecution: capabilities.execution,\n\t\tretained: capabilities.retained,\n\t});\n\n\t// Re-assert the original identity relationships before delivery so a caller\n\t// cannot observe a broken lineage even if a handle was tampered with.\n\tif (\n\t\tlevels.execution.identity !== levels.identity ||\n\t\tlevels.retained.execution !== levels.execution\n\t) {\n\t\tthrow new TypeError(\"causal capability lower handle mismatch\");\n\t}\n\n\trecipient(levels);\n}\n",
        "explanation": "The task asks for a handoff function that refuses to call the recipient unless the supplied FullCausalCapability is the exact issued object for the given Graph and expected CausalBinding, and that delivers the three levels without breaking their original identity relationships.\n\nDesign and evidence:\n\n1. Validation is delegated to the existing exact-issued-object check, `assertCausalCapabilities(graph, full, binding)` (packages/ts/src/solutions/causal-occurrence/capabilities.ts:66-90). It looks the capability up in the module-private `issued` WeakMap (capabilities.ts:45-51) and throws `TypeError(\"causal capability binding mismatch\")` when the root is absent (a structural copy or cast), when `root.graph !== graph` (different Graph), or when any of contract / implementationRevision / scope / epoch differ from the expected binding (capabilities.ts:72-82). It then checks each of the three level handles for the same instance and Graph (lineage, capabilities.ts:83-87) and finally the lower-handle identity relationships `full.execution.identity === full.identity` and `full.retained.execution === full.execution` (capabilities.ts:88-89). Because the check throws before the recipient is reached, the recipient is never called for a copy, a foreign Graph, or a mismatched binding. The README states the same rule: \"A capability validation failure must be handled before passing the capabilities to a recipient; a structural copy is not an issued object\" (examples/spending-alerts/README.md:41-42), and the framework example does exactly this before returning a level (examples/spending-alerts/causal-audience.examples.ts:17-25).\n\n2. The three levels are delivered as the original frozen handles taken from the supplied capability, not rebuilt. `createCausalCapabilities` freezes `identity`, `execution`, `retained` and `full` and registers each in the `issued` WeakMap with the same `instance` (capabilities.ts:122-146), so passing `capabilities.identity/execution/retained` preserves the original identity relationships. The extra guard in the snippet re-checks `execution.identity === identity` and `retained.execution === execution` before delivery, mirroring capabilities.ts:88-89, so a recipient can never observe a broken lineage.\n\n3. The snippet is a virtual file at examples/spending-alerts/__b121_entry_validation__.ts, so imports are relative from that directory: `../../packages/ts/src/graph/graph.js` and `../../packages/ts/src/solutions/causal-occurrence/capabilities.js`, matching the import style used by the example sources (e.g. causal-audience.examples.ts:2-7).\n\n4. Does possession of the execution level authorize an arbitrary I/O call? No. The execution level is a passive set of graph ports (terminals, conservation, causalQuiescence, plus the identity subset) — see ExecutionCapability at capabilities.ts:27-32. It exposes no write/open/close method. The README says \"Capabilities have no arbitrary I/O execution method\" (README.md:94) and the audience example comments \"an execution view grants no I/O method\" (causal-audience.examples.ts:17). Actual I/O is performed only by the owning host through a claimed resource lease, and only after the host's own final guard admits the exact request: the guard checks the current policy watermark, a single passing verification receipt, a single non-revoked grant bound to the exact proposal/receipt/grant/binding hash, the grant validity window, and inbox readiness with exactly one available slot (causal-focused-host.ts:404-431); otherwise the effect is completed as `cancelled` with no write (causal-focused-host.ts:432-438). The write itself goes through `lease.write(payload)` (causal-focused-host.ts:455), and the resource lease is created by `resource.claim()` inside `composeSpendingHost` (causal-focused-host.ts:96, 110), not by any capability. So the execution level is evidence/observation authority over the causal closure, not permission to perform arbitrary I/O; the README also notes that structural presence alone does not establish permission (README.md:122-123) and that the demo's writer is an offline simulation with `realInboxIO: false` (causal-graded-demo.ts:255-256).\n\nNo code was executed; this is a static reading of the supplied sources.",
